-- title:
--   Choice probability function of the additive random utility model
-- statement:
--   In the **additive random utility model** an agent chooses among alternatives $A = \{1, \dots, n\}$ with base payoffs $\pi = (\pi_1, \dots, \pi_n) \in \mathbb{R}^n$. Choosing $i$ also yields a stochastic payoff $\varepsilon_i$, where the random vector $\varepsilon = (\varepsilon_1, \dots, \varepsilon_n)$ has a **strictly positive density** $f : \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}$ that does not depend on $\pi$. Here this means: $f$ is continuous, $f(x) > 0$ for every $x \in \mathbb{R}^n$, and $\int_{\mathbb{R}^n} f(x)\,dx = 1$. The law of $\varepsilon$ is the probability measure $P(E) = \int_E f(x)\,dx$.
--
--   The agent picks the alternative with the highest total payoff, so the **choice probability function** $C : \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}^n$ is
--
--   $$
--   C_i(\pi) = P\big(\operatorname{argmax}_j\, \pi_j + \varepsilon_j = i\big) = P\big(\pi_j + \varepsilon_j < \pi_i + \varepsilon_i \text{ for all } j \neq i\big).
--   $$
--
--   The logit choice function $L_i(\pi) = \exp(\eta^{-1}\pi_i) / \sum_j \exp(\eta^{-1}\pi_j)$ is the best-known instance, generated by i.i.d. extreme-value shocks. The definition covers every strictly positive density; the theorems of this mission are about all of them at once.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Alternatives are indexed by `Fin n`. The event "$i$ is the argmax" is written with strict inequalities; ties $\pi_j + \varepsilon_j = \pi_i + \varepsilon_i$ lie on hyperplanes, which have probability zero under a density, so this is the paper's equation (1). The density is real-valued and the law is `volume.withDensity (fun x => ENNReal.ofReal (f x))` on `Fin n → ℝ`. Continuity of $f$ is how the paper's formula (4), which evaluates $f$ on hyperplanes, is read; strict positivity is required at every point.
-- source:
--   Hofbauer and Sandholm, On the Global Convergence of Stochastic Fictitious Play, Econometrica 70 (2002); authors' manuscript of February 21, 2002, p. 4, §2, eq. (1) and the paragraph before it

import Mathlib

open MeasureTheory

namespace StochFictPlay.DiscreteChoice

/-- `f : ℝⁿ → ℝ` is a strictly positive probability density of the utility-shock vector
`ε = (ε₁, …, εₙ)`: it is continuous, strictly positive at every point, and integrates to one
against Lebesgue measure. Continuity is how the formula (4) of Hofbauer–Sandholm (2002),
which evaluates `f` on hyperplanes, is read. -/
structure IsStrictlyPositiveDensity {n : ℕ} (f : (Fin n → ℝ) → ℝ) : Prop where
  continuous : Continuous f
  pos : ∀ x, 0 < f x
  lintegral_eq_one : ∫⁻ x, ENNReal.ofReal (f x) = 1

/-- The law of the shock vector `ε` with density `f`: Lebesgue measure on `ℝⁿ` weighted by `f`. -/
noncomputable def noiseLaw {n : ℕ} (f : (Fin n → ℝ) → ℝ) : Measure (Fin n → ℝ) :=
  volume.withDensity (fun x => ENNReal.ofReal (f x))

/-- The choice probability function (1) of the additive random utility model,
`Cᵢ(π) = P(argmaxⱼ πⱼ + εⱼ = i)`: the probability, under the law of `ε`, that alternative `i`
has strictly the highest total payoff `πᵢ + εᵢ`. Ties have probability zero because `ε` has a
density. Hofbauer–Sandholm (2002), p. 4, eq. (1). -/
noncomputable def choiceProb {n : ℕ} (f : (Fin n → ℝ) → ℝ) (π : Fin n → ℝ) (i : Fin n) : ℝ :=
  (noiseLaw f {e | ∀ j, j ≠ i → π j + e j < π i + e i}).toReal

end StochFictPlay.DiscreteChoice


